* VSBR, VMBR and changing memory (made up for testing the debugger).
* Row 1 shows HELLO, written with VMBW.
* Row 3 shows the first letter, read back with VSBR and written again with VSBW.
* Row 4 shows HELLO again: read back with VMBR and written with VMBW.
* Row 5 shows the digit stored in SCORE.
* To try changing memory: put a breakpoint on the line marked  <== BREAK HERE,
* right-click it, Edit Breakpoint, Hit Count, type 1. Debug it, and when it stops
* type   @SCORE = >0500   in the Debug Console. Press Continue: the digit becomes 5.
        DEF   START
        REF   VSBW,VSBR,VMBW,VMBR
        AORG  >A000

START   LWPI  WS
        LIMI  0

        LI    R0,>0000          ; row 1
        LI    R1,MSG
        LI    R2,5
        BLWP  @VMBW             ; write HELLO

        CLR   R0
        BLWP  @VSBR             ; read the first letter back into R1 (left byte)
        LI    R0,>0040
        BLWP  @VSBW             ; and write it on row 3

        CLR   R0
        LI    R1,BUF
        LI    R2,5
        BLWP  @VMBR             ; read HELLO back into BUF

        LI    R0,>0060
        LI    R1,BUF
        LI    R2,5
        BLWP  @VMBW             ; write BUF on row 4

LOOP    MOVB  @SCORE,R1         ; <== BREAK HERE
        AI    R1,>3000          ; 0 becomes the character '0'
        LI    R0,>0080
        BLWP  @VSBW             ; show it on row 5
        JMP   LOOP

SCORE   DATA  >0000
MSG     TEXT  'HELLO'
        EVEN
BUF     BSS   8
WS      BSS   32
        END   START
